ansitive identity principles.\nclaim-grounding-and-essence-can-both-be-analyzed-in-terms-of-generalized-identity-yiel--e2abdab353f9\tGeneralized identity unifies metaphysics\tGrounding and essence can both be analyzed in terms of generalized identity, yielding a unified and uniform account of how the two metaphysical notions interact.\nclaim-if-being-f-grounds-being-g-then-the-fact-that-being-f-necessitates-being-g-can-b--685ae4dcbfb4\tGrounding yields necessitation\tIf being F grounds being G, then the fact that being F necessitates being G can be derived from what it is to be G.\nclaim-necessary-entailment-or-equivalence-is-not-sufficient-for-essence-because-genera--162946956228\tNecessity underdetermines essence\tNecessary entailment or equivalence is not sufficient for essence, because generalized identity and grounding can distinguish essential structure from merely necessary coextension.\nclaim-objectual-essence-can-be-accounted-for-through-generic-essence-so-a-feature-is-w--bd5daa95f779\tObjectual essence tracks identity\tObjectual essence can be accounted for through generic essence, so a feature is what an object is in full or in part according to whether it is what being identical to that object is in full or in part.\nclaim-partial-generic-essence-is-captured-by-conjunctive-partial-generalized-identity--13ec1cd27c1f\tPartial essence is conjunctive\tPartial generic essence is captured by conjunctive partial generalized identity: being F is partially what it is to be G exactly when being G is being both F and some H.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
